# notation/leanix/factsheets - What is in the LeanIX inventory, and how complete?
#
# Answers: fact sheets with their type, status, completion ratio and lifecycle
# phase.
#
# Does NOT prove: that a fact sheet describes a system that exists, nor that a high
# completion ratio means the content is right. Completion measures how many fields
# were filled, not whether they were filled correctly.
#
# A fact sheet is a RECORD ABOUT a thing, not the thing. Treating one as the
# application it describes merges a register entry with its subject and inflates
# every count downstream. That is why cross-source identity to a fact sheet should
# be skos:exactMatch and never owl:sameAs - see core/identity-audit, and design
# decision DD-11.
#
# One row per fact sheet, with lifecycle phases concatenated. A fact sheet has several
# phases by design - that is what a lifecycle is - so a row per phase multiplied the
# inventory by however many phases each entry recorded, and an inventory that overstates
# its own size is worse than one that omits a column. The remaining fields are grouping
# keys because LeanIX carries at most one of each; if a dataset carries more, those rows
# still separate, which is visible rather than silent.
#
# Parameters: LIMIT
{{PREFIXES}}
SELECT ?factSheet ?label ?type ?status ?completion
       (GROUP_CONCAT(DISTINCT ?phase; separator=", ") AS ?phases) ?url
WHERE {
  {{GRAPH_OPEN:semantic}}
    ?factSheet a lmm:FactSheet .
    OPTIONAL { ?factSheet {{PATH:label}} ?label }
    OPTIONAL { ?factSheet dct:type ?type }
    OPTIONAL { ?factSheet lmm:factSheetStatus ?status }
    OPTIONAL { ?factSheet lmm:completion ?completion }
    OPTIONAL {
      ?factSheet lmm:hasLifecyclePhase ?phaseNode .
      OPTIONAL { ?phaseNode {{PATH:label}} ?phase }
    }
    OPTIONAL { ?factSheet schema:url ?url }
  {{GRAPH_CLOSE}}
}
GROUP BY ?factSheet ?label ?type ?status ?completion ?url
ORDER BY ?type ?label
LIMIT {{LIMIT}}
